+## 3. Pull: populate `wfa_*` from L&D's analytics endpoint
+
+### 3.1 The call
+
+```
+GET {APP_URL}/modules/learning/api/outbound/get-workforce-analytics.php
+X-API-Key: ld_b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c9
+```
+
+Response is `{"success":true,"data":{ … }}` (the field-by-field example is in §7.2).
+
+**⚠️ The docblock advertises `department_id`, `date_from`, `date_to`, but the current code ignores all
+three** — it always returns organisation-wide, all-time aggregates. Filter and aggregate on your side,
+or ask L&D to implement them. Do not build UI that claims those filters work.
+
+### 3.2 Where each block lands, with the real destination columns
+
+| Payload block | Destination table | Columns to write | Upsert key |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `enrollment_stats`, `completion_trend`, `top_courses` | `wfa_employee_metrics` | `metric_date`, `total_employees` (← `total_learners`), `total_departments` | `unique_metric_date` (`metric_date`) |
+| `skill_gap_analysis[]` (+ `skill_snapshots[]` for `department_id`) | `wfa_skill_gap_analysis` | `department_id` (**NOT NULL**), `skill_name`, `employees_with_skill` (← `employees_with_skill` / `acquired_count` SUM), `employees_needing_training`, `skill_gap_percentage`, `priority_level`, `last_assessed` | `unique_skill_gap` (`department_id`,`skill_name`) |
+| `skill_snapshots[]` | `wfa_skill_gap_analysis` (per-learner detail → roll up per department) or your own detail table | `learner_id`, `department_name`, `position_name`, `total_skills`, `acquired_count`, `gap_count`, `acquired_skills`, `gap_skills` | — |
+| `risk_summary.bands[]` | `wfa_at_risk_employees_summary` | `risk_level` (`high`\|`medium`\|`low`), `count`, `avg_risk_score`, `percentage` | no key — replace the day's rows |
+| `risk_summary.at_risk_learners[]` | `wfa_risk_assessment` | `employee_id`, `risk_level` (band from the score: ≥70 `high`, 40–69 `medium`, else `low`), `risk_score`, `risk_factors` (**JSON string — the column has a `json_valid` CHECK**), `low_engagement_flag`, `last_assessment_date` (← `snapshot_date`), `notes` | no unique key — see the warning below |
+| `expiring_certificates[]` | `wfa_reports` | `report_name` (e.g. `Expiring certifications — L&D`), `report_type` = `custom`, `report_date`, `report_data` (JSON of the array), `filters_applied` (JSON) | `(report_name, report_date, report_type)` checked in code |
+| `top_courses[]` | `wfa_performance_distribution` is a reasonable home for course-level completion spreads; otherwise `wfa_reports` | `metric_date`, `performance_level`, `employee_count`, `percentage`, `department_breakdown` (JSON) | check `(metric_date, performance_level)` in code |
+| `total_certificates`, `knowledge_transfer` | `wfa_reports` (`report_data` JSON) | as above | as above |
+
+`wfa_risk_assessment` has **no unique key on `employee_id`** — a naive insert-every-run consumer grows a
+new row per employee per day. Use one of: update the row where `employee_id` + `last_assessment_date`
+already match, or add `unique(employee_id, last_assessment_date)` in your migration. Same class of
+problem for `wfa_at_risk_employees_summary`: `DELETE FROM … WHERE` then re-insert the current bands.
+
+### 3.3 Cadence and freshness
+
+- L&D's own snapshot job (`ld_engagement_snapshot`, `ld_skill_snapshot`) runs **daily at 06:00**;
+  `risk_summary.snapshot_date` and `skill_snapshots[].snapshot_date` tell you which run you got.
+- Everything else (`enrollment_stats`, `completion_trend`, `top_courses`, `expiring_certificates`,
+  `total_certificates`, `knowledge_transfer`) is computed **live at call time**.
+- **Recommended: pull once daily at ~07:00**, after L&D's snapshot, and store the payload's own
+  `snapshot_date` values instead of `CURDATE()` so your dashboards can say "as of 2026-09-25".
+- Pull from a scheduled job (or on page load with a "last synced" timestamp), never on every render.
+
+### 3.4 Your puller, in outline
+
+```php
+// 1. GET with the ld_ key (§3.1) — same helper shape as §2.5, method GET.
+// 2. $data = $decoded['data'];
+// 3. One transaction per block, keyed as in §3.2:
